\begin{question}
\textbf{F-distribution}

Let $W_1\sim\chi^2_3$ and $W_2\sim\chi^2_{10}$ be independent, and let $Z_1, Z_2$ be independent standard normal random variables. Which random variable has an $F_{3,10}$ distribution?
\begin{answerlist}
\item $\dfrac{W_1/3}{W_2/10}$
\item $\dfrac{W_1}{W_2}$
\item $\dfrac{W_1+W_2}{13}$
\item $\dfrac{W_2/10}{W_1/3}$
\item $\dfrac{Z_1}{Z_2}$
\end{answerlist}
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
\begin{answerlist}
\item True. The ratio of two independent chi-squared random variables, each divided by its degrees of freedom, is $F$-distributed; the numerator's degrees of freedom come first.
\item False. Both chi-squared variables must be divided by their degrees of freedom.
\item False. A sum of independent chi-squared variables is again chi-squared distributed ($\chi^2_{13}$); dividing by 13 does not give an $F$-distribution.
\item False. This ratio has an $F_{10,3}$ distribution (the order of the degrees of freedom matters).
\item False. The $F$-distribution is built from chi-squared (i.e., squared normal) variables, not from normal ones.
\end{answerlist}
\end{solution}

